    <h2>What's driving the increases?</h2>
    <p>Three patterns stand out in our tracking. First, treats have risen faster in percentage terms than you might expect for low-cost items — the Blizzard's ~28% climb outpaces the burger line. Second, chicken has been the fastest riser overall (+33% on the 4-piece basket), mirroring poultry and frying-oil cost pressure across the whole fast-food sector. Third, DQ has leaned on bundles — the $7 Meal Deal and 2-for-$5 Mix &amp; Match — to keep an affordable headline price on the board even as à-la-carte items climbed. If you're price-sensitive, the deals page is where DQ has effectively "hidden" its old price points.</p>

    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <p>Figures on this page are <strong>estimated U.S. national averages compiled independently by this site</strong>. We build them from franchise menu boards and receipts shared publicly, archived menu-site captures, and DQ app pricing sampled across multiple regions, then average them. They are not official Dairy Queen figures — DQ franchisees set their own prices, and any single location can be meaningfully above or below these averages. Historical figures are our best reconstruction and are revised if better data surfaces. This page is updated every January and July; it was last updated July 2026.</p>

    <p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--color-text-muted)">The Queen Dairy Menu is an independent consumer reference site and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Dairy Queen® or International Dairy Queen, Inc.</p>

